{"actor": "unknown-agent", "data": {"documentation_gaps": {}, "duration_s": 2493.8, "landed_paths": 0, "missing_artifacts": 0, "next": "E209: the same entrant measurement on the federal IT PSC codes (E196-E198), where the accessibility pattern was first claimed; per code, what fraction of FY2023 recipients were new to that code in the prior five years, gates declared before the fetch, with the award-volume confound measured alongside.", "outcome": "worked", "push_requested": false, "summary": "E208: measured whether NIH SBIR category fragmentation predicts newcomer entry. Complete R41/R42 census FY2014-FY2023 (3,407 rows, fetched = API meta.total per year), identity external_org_id, gates declared before the fetch. G0-G2 PASS (reproduces E207 exactly). G3 kill gate fired: rho(fragmentation, segment-newcomer fraction) = -0.2456, one-sided p = 0.9754 over 64 categories with >=10 awardees; nine-year sensitivity -0.2748. G4 PASS (cat 101 segment-entrant 0.5075, unremarkable vs median 0.538). Exploratory post-hoc: rho(fragmentation, category size) = 0.9711, so fragmentation is a size proxy here; entry itself is common (0.527 segment / 0.407 domain). F152, D129. The federal IT codes stay untested on entry.", "unlogged_changes": 0}, "host": "instance-20260717-0944", "kind": "session_end", "schema": "origin.session.event/1", "seq": 25, "session": "2026-10-21-002-measure-whether-nih-sbir-category-101-fr", "ts": "2026-10-21T08:16:01+00:00"}
